1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,028
The trial run had a new shape: one afternoon, one line of questions, and a small group of volunteers.

2
00:00:08,028 --> 00:00:12,062
Each carried a soft pencil and a printed page.

3
00:00:12,062 --> 00:00:20,209
The engineer who designed the form was curious about a single number — how many people could find the answer without help.

4
00:00:20,209 --> 00:00:25,407
“Few,” she said, when the first results came in.

5
00:00:25,407 --> 00:00:28,566
“That is the problem.”

6
00:00:28,566 --> 00:00:31,940
The performance was better than they had feared.

7
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:44,056
A humble typist, an ace with a needle and a length of silk ribbon, fixed the broken sign in ten minutes, and a ham sandwich with butter bought her a grateful hour.

8
00:00:44,056 --> 00:00:51,644
A girl in a cat sweater asked whether the map worked for someone who could not read; nobody answered.

9
00:00:51,644 --> 00:00:55,168
“That is the catch,” Maya said.

10
00:00:55,168 --> 00:01:07,844
“We wrote for the wrong reader.” A rumor went round the library that the group had taken money from a bakery; the toast and butter tasted better than that story.

11
00:01:07,844 --> 00:01:11,813
The content of the next version had to change.

12
00:01:11,813 --> 00:01:20,935
They would compose it in three languages, diversify the examples, and add a line for readers who preferred pictures.

13
00:01:20,935 --> 00:01:24,194
“It is awkward,” Lin admitted.

14
00:01:24,194 --> 00:01:41,453
“It is also accessible.” A historical exhibit on Christianity gave them an unexpected lesson: a manuscript copied by hand by a Christian scribe whose name was never recorded, with one old note about Christ in the margin.

15
00:01:41,453 --> 00:01:46,566
“That is the pioneer of our profession,” the librarian said.

16
00:01:46,566 --> 00:01:53,859
“Efficiency came later.” Chalk dust still lay on the shelf beneath it.

17
00:01:53,859 --> 00:01:57,503
Recognition arrived in small forms.

18
00:01:57,503 --> 00:02:04,490
A professor praised their “purity of method” and then misspelled Maya's name in the citation.

19
00:02:04,490 --> 00:02:07,350
A fiber of doubt remained.

20
00:02:07,350 --> 00:02:11,263
“I am not hopeless about it,” she said.

21
00:02:11,263 --> 00:02:23,065
“I am just not finished.” An innocent remark about a clumsy diagram started an hour-long argument, and her finger stayed on the page the whole time.

22
00:02:23,065 --> 00:02:29,648
A responsive reader, they learned, beats ten quiet ones.

23
00:02:29,648 --> 00:02:37,080
A mammal, a pizza box, and a book of riddles shared one table by the end of the week.

24
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:43,418
Their flexible schedule allowed one more trial; other volunteers signed up.

25
00:02:43,418 --> 00:02:50,151
Not every session was useful; most were not, and nobody could say quite why.

26
00:02:50,151 --> 00:02:53,775
“Write it down anyway,” she said.

27
00:02:53,775 --> 00:02:59,593
“Again.” Nobody knew how much the answer depended on luck.

28
00:02:59,593 --> 00:03:07,836
It was, in his words, a trifle: three hundred grams of paper and a million intentions.

29
00:03:07,836 --> 00:03:12,054
“Some things are infectious,” Maya said.

30
00:03:12,054 --> 00:03:14,979
“Curiosity is one of them.

31
00:03:14,979 --> 00:03:23,376
Wake me when the next batch arrives.” Many notes were his, and the study would carry all of them.

32
00:03:23,376 --> 00:03:31,644
She smiled at the clumsy sentence he had just written; the wrinkle beside her eye showed she had slept badly.

33
00:03:31,644 --> 00:03:35,630
“No,” she said.
